% lomo_calendar.le — the obligations of a LOMO description through time,
% in Logical English for LPS.
%
% The timeless reading of a LOMO description is LE2's library lomo.le: who
% is bound, by which date. This library is the reading through time: a duty
% starts when its trigger happens to a party, ends when the party performs
% the action in time, and is breached when the clock passes its due day. A
% run shows, day by day, which duties are open, met and breached: the
% "deadline calendar" of LOMO's requirements document, running.
%
% A program of a case extends this library. The LOMO translator of LE2
% writes such programs (migration/lomo/lomo_lps.pl): the facts of who is
% bound come from the timeless reading of the register and the case, and
% each date of the case is a day number, because LE for LPS counts days, not
% calendar dates. What follows a breach is not in LOMO; a program can add it
% as one more law.
%
% Copyright Miguel Calejo; Apache License 2.0.

the target language is: lps.

the events are:
    *an event* happens to *a party* on day *a day*; known as lomo_happens.
    *a party* performs *an action* on day *a day*; known as lomo_performs.
    the clock reaches day *a day*; known as lomo_clock.

the fluents are:
    *a party* owes *an action* under *a rule* by day *a due day*; known as lomo_owes.
    *a party* met *an action* under *a rule*; known as lomo_met.
    *a party* breached *an action* under *a rule* by day *a due day*; known as lomo_breached.

the templates are:
    *a rule* binds *a party* to *an action* after *an event* within *a duration*.
    the period *a duration* after day *a day* is day *a later day*.

the knowledge base lomo calendar includes:

% A trigger happens: the duty starts, with its due day.
when an event happens to a party on day a day
    and a rule binds the party to an action after the event within a duration
    and the period the duration after day the day is day a due day
then the party owes the action under the rule by day the due day.

% The action, in time: the duty is met and ends.
when a party performs an action on day a day
    and the party owes the action under a rule by day a due day
    and the day <= the due day
then the party met the action under the rule
    and it is not the case that the party owes the action under the rule by day the due day.

% The clock passes the due day: the duty is breached and ends.
when the clock reaches day a day
    and a party owes an action under a rule by day a due day
    and the day > the due day
then the party breached the action under the rule by day the due day
    and it is not the case that the party owes the action under the rule by day the due day.
